<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rashid Rida</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Rashid Rida, Muḥammad Rashīd Riḍā</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Rashid Rida was an Islamic scholar, reformer, theologian and revivalist, born in al-Qalamoun, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, in 1865. He joined his mentor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and in 1898 began publishing al-Manar, through which he became one of the most influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> jurists of his generation and a leader of the Arab Salafi movement. An early Salafist, he later turned from rationalist leanings to a stricter scripturalist approach, supported the Wahhabi movement and revived the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-taymiyya">Ibn Taymiyyah</a>. He opposed secularism, nationalism and European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> and championed a pan-Islamist program to re-establish a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashid-rida.jpg" alt="Rashid Rida" width="330" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuhammadRashidRida.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rashid Rida was born in 1865 in al-Qalamoun, in the Beirut Vilayet, into a distinguished Sunni Shafi'i clerical family. The family lived on a limited holding of olive trees and the fees of its scholars, and for several generations had charge of the al-Qalamoun mosque, where his father was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>. They were Sayyids who claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>. Rida met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a> as an exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> in the mid-1880s and came to regard him as his mentor. In 1897 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> to study under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a>, but after al-Afghani's death that year, which Rida suspected the Hamidian administration of causing, he left to rejoin Abduh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> the two began the monthly al-Manar, of which Rida was chief editor and owner until his death in 1935. His study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-taymiyya">Ibn Taymiyya</a> led him to revulsion against folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>, criticism of taqlid and the wish to revive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> studies. In 1897 he helped found the Ottoman Consultative Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, which called for Islamic unity under Ottomanism and condemned Hamidian rule; it disbanded after the Young Turk Revolution of 1908, and Rida became a vocal critic of the Young Turks. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he negotiated with the British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> for a united Islamic state, but he distrusted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and more so after the Sykes–Picot Agreement.</p><p>After the war he turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>, whom he saw as the best candidate to free the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> from Hashemite rule, and he welcomed his campaigns of 1924 and 1925. He took part in preparations for the 1926 Islamic Congress for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and was a prominent delegate at the Pan-Islamic Congress that Ibn Saud convened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> the same year, drafting protocols and the king's opening address. No significant resolutions were passed there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In The Caliphate or the Supreme Imamate (1922–23) Rida proposed a comprehensive pan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamism">Islamism</a> and urged Muslims to shun nationalism. After the 1900s he abandoned his early rationalism for a stricter scripturalism, supported the Wahhabis and revived the works of Ibn Taymiyyah, and he gave naql, textual sources, precedence over aql. He condemned tawassul and the veneration of graves as polytheism, and by the 1920s regarded Western-educated secularists as his chief opponents, attacking Ali Abdel Raziq among others. He grew sharply critical of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a>. His commentary on the Qur'an, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Abduh</a>, reached sura Yusuf.</p><p>Rida died on the way back from Suez to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> after seeing off King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>, and he died in debt, having put most of his money into publishing. Historians regard him as one of the ideological forefathers of later Islamist movements and as a link between classical theories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> and the twentieth-century idea of the Islamic state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rashid Rida” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Muqtataf moved to Cairo in 1884; Jurji Zaydan founded al-Hilal there in 1892, the year Hind Nawfal launched al-Fatat in Alexandria; and <b>Rashid Rida</b> began al-Manar in 1898.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the 19th century, as European colonial powers dismembered most of the Ottoman Empire, Jamal al-Din al-Afghani (1837–97), Muhammad Abduh (1849–1905) and <b>Rashid Rida</b> (1865–1935) preached Islamic alternatives to that decline.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Al-Qalamoun, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.38,35.78&amp;year=1910" aria-label="Al-Qalamoun on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5460 918 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rashid Rida"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5910 1188h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5910 1188h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.38,35.78&amp;year=1910">What was here in 1910? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
